<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bambatha Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bambatha Rebellion was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> uprising against British rule and taxation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colony-of-natal">Colony of Natal</a> in 1906, sparked by a new poll tax. It was led by Bambatha kaMancinza, a chief of the Zondi, and fought largely in the forests of the Nkandla region. Colonial forces crushed it, killing several thousand Africans, and it was the last armed Zulu resistance to colonial rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bambatha-rebellion.jpg" alt="Bambatha Rebellion" width="320" height="140" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZuluWarriors.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> ended in 1902, European employers in Natal struggled to recruit Black labourers, who were drawn to the gold mines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a>. The colonial authorities added a poll tax of one pound to the existing hut tax to push <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> men into wage work. The tax fell hardest on poorer households, at a time when European landowners were evicting African tenants onto crowded reserves and an epidemic of 1896 to 1897 had killed 90 per cent of local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>.</p><p>The return in 1897 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinuzulu-kacetshwayo">Dinuzulu kaCetshwayo</a> from exile on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">Saint Helena</a> fed rumours that he was planning a rising. Some chiefs and elders tried to prevent one, but many young men were united by the tax. Demonstrations at tax stations led to the deaths of two police constables on 8 February 1906, and martial law followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Bambatha, suspected of joining other chiefs in protesting the tax, ignored a summons to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-del-norte">Greytown</a> for fear of arrest and went to consult Dinuzulu. Returning to the Mpanza Valley, he found that the government had deposed him. From 3 April he led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> attacks from a base in the Nkandla forest. Natal, aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and Cape colonies, raised a force of 4,316 men under Colonel Duncan McKenzie, which surrounded the rebels at Mome Gorge on 10 June and attacked them at sunrise. Bambatha was reported killed, though his supporters believed he had escaped to Portuguese Mozambique.</p><p>Chief Meseni carried on the fight in the lower Thukela Valley from 13 June to 11 July. The bloodiest phase came in the rest of 1906, when colonial forces carried out indiscriminate operations against any perceived resistance. More than 7,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulus</a> were imprisoned and 4,000 flogged, and 36 colonial soldiers died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Suppression cost Natal £883,576. The rising strengthened support among white colonists for uniting the colonies of the region to maintain white supremacy, and the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was formed in 1910. Mohandas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karamchand-gandhi">Karamchand Gandhi</a>, then a lawyer in South Africa, led a stretcher-bearer corps of 21 Indian volunteers for the colonial side; by 1927 he called the campaign no war but a man hunt. At the centenary in 2006 Bambatha was declared a national hero, and a DNA test of his alleged body gave no definite answer.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bambatha Rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Bambatha rebellion</b> against a poll tax was crushed in 1906.</blockquote><a href="/state/natal-colony">Natal Colony</a></li></ul></section>
